\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\paragraph{Methodological pitfalls.}
\label{sec:pitfalls}
Lead-lag analysis in iterative model collapse is fragile along three axes, each documented with data from \S\ref{sec:regime}--\ref{sec:dose_response}.
\emph{Method sensitivity}: five detection methods applied to identical data ($\alpha{=}1.0$, 8 validated canary$\to$downstream pairs, direction-checked) yield forward significance rates spanning 0\% to 75\%, with inter-method agreement at $\kappa{=}0.185$ and naive cross-correlation reaching 75\% null-control FPR.
\emph{Spurious regression}: first-differencing to remove shared trends~\cite{granger1974spurious} eliminates canary$\to$perplexity associations ($F{\approx}0$, $p{=}0.99$) while preserving canary$\to$diversity (24/24$\to$24/24), exposing an entire analysis channel as trend-driven rather than temporally precedent.
\emph{Regime dependence}: the same Granger test finds 0/8 pairs significant through $\alpha{=}0.45$ but 6/8 at $\alpha{=}0.50$, a categorical shift at a 5-percentage-point boundary that a single-$\alpha$ study would miss entirely.
These pitfalls motivate systematic method comparison rather than reliance on any single detection approach.

\paragraph{Multi-Method Consensus Test (MMCT).}
\label{sec:mmct}
We compare five lead-lag detection methods through MMCT, a weighted consensus protocol~\cite{simonsohn2020specification,scheffer2009early} calibrated against null-control ($\alpha{=}0.0$) data where any detection is a false positive.
Each method receives FPR-inverse weight; the weighted score yields formal $p$-values from the empirical null distribution (Algorithm in Appendix~\ref{app:mmct}).
MMCT identifies PermG as the only method combining zero null-control FPR with consistent detection; this ranking holds under all four weight schemes tested, from equal weighting to FPR-inverse caps (Appendix~\ref{app:weight_robustness}).

\paragraph{Permutation Granger causality.}
PermG tests whether past values of canary metric $X$ improve prediction of downstream metric $Y$ beyond $Y$'s own history; Granger precedence establishes temporal predictive improvement, not true causality~\cite{shojaie2022granger}.
We apply 5{,}000 permutations~\citep{phipson2010permutation} on first-differenced series with Benjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q{=}0.05$ across 8 validated canary$\to$downstream pairs (2 canaries $\times$ 4 downstream metrics).
Three properties distinguish PermG: a nonparametric null distribution robust to the short non-stationary series typical of iterative training~\cite{boettiger2012limits}, built-in directionality (forward and reverse tested separately), and a hyperparameter-free test statistic.
The remaining four methods each exhibit a specific limitation: naive cross-correlation has 75\% null-control FPR; differenced cross-correlation detects bidirectionally without directional separation; threshold onset is power-limited at $T{=}11$ under corrected $3\sigma$ calibration (0/8 significant in 8 of 12 cross-architecture conditions); Toda--Yamamoto~\cite{toda1995statistical} provides supplementary detection with reduced power from degree-of-freedom costs of lag augmentation (directional assignment under verification).
Per-method dose-response profiles, Shapley value analysis, and the full MMCT algorithm appear in the Appendix.

\subsection{Signal-Times-Resolution Interpretive Lens}
\label{sec:theory}

We apply standard statistical power scaling to PermG, interpreted through a Signal$\times$Resolution (S$\times$R) decomposition.
Detectability of canary$\to$downstream temporal precedence depends on two competing factors:
\begin{equation}
    \text{Detectability}(\alpha) \;\propto\; S(\alpha) \times R(\alpha),
    \label{eq:detectability}
\end{equation}
where $S(\alpha)$ is signal strength (magnitude of distributional shift, monotonically increasing with $\alpha$) and $R(\alpha)$ is temporal resolution (the generational gap between canary and downstream responses).
$R(\alpha)$ is non-monotonic: at low $\alpha$, entropy and diversity respond in different generations ($R$ large); at $\alpha{\approx}0.60$, relaxation time matching compresses both responses into a single generation ($R \to 0$); at high $\alpha$, the severity of entropy collapse outpaces the diversity cascade, restoring the generational offset.
The product $S \times R$ predicts four regimes: \emph{blind} ($\alpha \leq 0.45$; $S$ below detection floor), \emph{onset} ($\alpha \approx 0.50$; $S$ crosses threshold while $R$ remains high), \emph{synchronous dip} ($\alpha \approx 0.60$; $R \to 0$ suppresses detection despite rising $S$), and \emph{detection} ($\alpha \geq 0.75$; $S$ dominates).
Full empirical confirmation appears in \S\ref{sec:regime} and \S\ref{sec:dose_response}.

Under a permutation test framework, detection power scales as $\mathrm{Power}(\alpha, T) = \Phi\bigl(S \cdot R \cdot \sqrt{T} / \sigma_{\mathrm{noise}}\bigr)$, where $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF and $\sqrt{T}$ is a conservative lower bound from standard power analysis.
Critically, $\alpha$ drives detection through the signal term $S(\alpha)$; $T$ provides sample size, not signal power.
The S$\times$R decomposition predicts that detection count is T-invariant when detected pairs are already saturated ($F \gg F_{\text{crit}}$) and undetected pairs are structural nulls ($F \approx 1$): increasing $T$ cannot flip either class.
The sharp detection onset at $\alpha_c$ (at $T{=}10$) is consistent with a power threshold: when $S \cdot R \cdot \sqrt{T}$ first exceeds the detection floor, power undergoes a steep onset from near-zero to near-one.
$T{=}20$ validates this prediction across four contamination levels: PermG yields 6/8 at $\alpha \in \{0.50, 0.75, 1.0\}$ and 0/8 at $\alpha{=}0.60$, all identical to $T{=}10$; six diversity-based pairs are consistently significant ($F{=}449$--$2947$) while two mauve-based pairs show $F{<}2$ regardless of sequence length (\S\ref{sec:dose_response}).
Sub-threshold FDR $p{=}0.059$--$0.091$ at $\alpha{=}0.45$ ($T{=}10$) is consistent with proximity to the power boundary.

\subsection{Cascade Ordering Theory}
\label{sec:cascade}

Under mode amplification---the assumption that MLE on peaked distributions concentrates probability toward high-frequency tokens~\cite{shumailov2024curse,dohmatob2024tale}---we derive a formal ordering of metric responses (proofs in Appendix~\ref{app:cascade_theory}).

\begin{theorem}[Entropy leads diversity]\label{thm:entropy_leads}
Under mode amplification: \textup{(a)} entropy can decrease while the $\epsilon$-effective support $S_\epsilon(p) {=} |\{i: p_i > \epsilon\}|$ (hence distinct-$n$) is unchanged; \textup{(b)} if distinct-$n$ decreases, entropy has already decreased.
\end{theorem}

The asymmetry arises because entropy responds to any mass redistribution (first-order), while diversity changes only when a token probability crosses the generation threshold~$\epsilon$.
The margin $\gamma = \min_{i: p_i > \epsilon}(p_i - \epsilon)$ quantifies how much shift diversity absorbs silently.

\begin{proposition}[Diversity lag $\geq 1$ generation]\label{prop:div_lag}
If the per-generation perturbation satisfies $\alpha\|\mathcal{T}(p_t) - p_t\|_\infty < \gamma(p_t, \epsilon)$ through entropy onset $t_H$, then diversity onset $t_D \geq t_H + 1$.
\end{proposition}

ECE co-moves with entropy at zero lag: under mode amplification, peakier conditionals simultaneously decrease per-token entropy and increase confidence; with accuracy approximately stationary in early collapse, $\Delta\mathrm{ECE} \approx \Delta C > 0$ whenever $\Delta\bar{H} < 0$ (qualitative argument; Appendix~\ref{app:cascade_theory}).
The predicted cascade $\{H, \mathrm{ECE}\} \to \mathrm{Distinct}$-$n$ is confirmed in 15/15 experiments at $\alpha \geq 0.30$: entropy and ECE onset at generation~1, distinct-$n$ at generation~2 ($t_H{=}t_{\mathrm{ECE}}{=}1$, $t_D{=}2$ uniformly; \S\ref{sec:regime}); five low-$\alpha$ experiments show no detectable onset, leaving the ordering undefined below the detection floor.
The cascade ordering also explains PermG's directional selectivity: because entropy \emph{leads} diversity, forward tests (canary$\to$diversity) detect temporal precedence while reverse tests find none (6/8 forward vs.\ 0/8 reverse at $\alpha \geq 0.50$).

\paragraph{Calibration.}
At $\alpha{=}0.0$ (null control), PermG yields 0\% FPR across 3~seeds (0/24 pairs; Rule-of-Three 95\% CI: [0\%, 12.5\%]); at $\alpha{=}1.0$, all 6 effect-size reference pairs are recovered (0\% FNR, 4~seeds).
Cross-architecture validation confirms PermG ${\geq}6/8$ in 13/15 conditions (\S\ref{sec:generalization}); full calibration details in the Appendix.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Granger precedence is not causation.}
Granger causality tests predictive improvement, not true causation~\cite{granger1969investigating,shojaie2022granger}: an unobserved process could independently drive both token entropy and diversity metrics.
Our central claim is scoped accordingly---``token entropy Granger-precedes diversity collapse''---and bivariate tests cannot exclude confounders.

\paragraph{Entropy--diversity coupling.}
Token entropy and distinct-$n$ share mechanical coupling (distributional concentration constrains $n$-gram diversity), but this would produce \emph{synchronous} co-movement (lag~0), not the consistent one-generation temporal lead we observe (Figure~\ref{fig:metric_trajectory}).
First-differencing rules out shared trends: the canary$\to$diversity channel survives ($24/24 \to 24/24$) while canary$\to$perplexity vanishes entirely, scoping all canary claims to diversity metrics only (Appendix~\ref{app:channel_effect_size}).
The temporal lead is formalized in \S\ref{sec:cascade}: entropy is a first-order indicator while diversity requires a discrete threshold crossing, producing a ${\geq}1$-generation lag under mode amplification; partial mechanical coupling cannot be fully excluded.

\paragraph{Time-series sufficiency.}
With $T{=}10$ (11 data points), our analyses operate near the lower bound of standard methods~\cite{boettiger2012limits}.
Uniform T-invariance across four contamination levels ($\alpha{=}0.45$ pending) provides the strongest evidence that $T{=}10$ suffices: doubling to $T{=}20$ produces identical PermG outcomes at every tested $\alpha$, confirming that effective sample size is not the binding constraint (\S\ref{sec:theory}).

\paragraph{Regime interpretation.}
The four-regime dose-response parallels hormetic patterns in toxicology~\cite{calabrese2003hormesis}, where opposing dose-dependent mechanisms produce non-monotonic responses.
At $\alpha{\approx}0.60$, temporal resolution $R \to 0$ suppresses detection despite rising signal, analogous to zero-lag Granger causality failure in synchronous systems.
A mutual information analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:mi_analysis}) confirms this: MI follows a V-shaped curve with an information-theoretic decorrelation zone near $\alpha{=}0.25$ (${\approx}0.02$~bits), remaining positive throughout the blind zone ($0.01$--$0.14$~bits at $\alpha \leq 0.45$) with no discontinuity at $\alpha_c$.
This distinction is important: our sharp onset is a \emph{statistical detection} phenomenon (PermG power crossing a threshold), not an information-theoretic phase transition---canary--diversity coupling exists continuously across $\alpha$, but falls below PermG's detection floor in the blind zone.

\paragraph{Cross-architecture scope.}
The two non-confirming conditions (SmolLM3-3B 3/8, Pythia-1.4B 2/8) reflect distinct failure modes: canary metric splitting (entropy 0/4 vs.\ ECE 3/4) and seed-dependent sensitivity (1.4B detects at $\alpha{=}0.50$ via ECE canary, suggesting canary metric choice mediates cross-scale sensitivity); C4 exhibits preliminary bidirectional coupling (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Lead-lag analysis in iterative training collapse is far more fragile than assumed: method choice alone inflates spurious-signal rates to 75\% null-control FPR, and regime dependence causes the same test to shift from significant to null.
PermG identifies token entropy as a one-generation-ahead canary for diversity collapse, validated across 15 model-seed combinations spanning six families (345M--6.9B) with detection in 13 cases (87\%).
Canary-based predictive cessation reduces peak perplexity by 58\%; extended discussion in Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Model collapse and canary metrics.}
Iterative training collapse was formalized by \citet{shumailov2024curse} and \citet{alemohammad2024mad}, who showed that recursively replacing real data with model-generated text causes progressive tail erosion; \citet{schaeffer2025position} systematize eight distinct notions of ``collapse'' that this literature conflates.
Theoretical analyses have sharpened these results: \citet{dohmatob2024tale} recast collapse as a change in scaling-law exponents, \citet{dohmatob2025strong} proved that collapse can occur even at vanishingly small synthetic fractions, \citet{seddik2025statistical} provide a statistical analysis of iterative contamination dynamics, and \citet{marchi2025challenging} challenge the universality of collapse under diverse training conditions.
Several works have proposed monitoring metrics: \citet{du2026geometric} observe that calibration error rises before accuracy degrades, \citet{du2025scales} report ECE increases at 30\% contamination before accuracy drops, \citet{gambetta2024surplexity} introduces surplexity as a collapse detector, and \citet{drayson2025detection} show that machine-generated text detection can prevent collapse, and \citet{li2025verification} propose verification-based mitigation.
We differ from the closest predecessor, \citet{du2026geometric}, in four respects: (i)~we compare an entire family of canary metrics; (ii)~we cross-validate across five statistical methods; (iii)~we map detectability as a function of contamination regime; and (iv)~we treat method-selection sensitivity itself as a primary finding.

\paragraph{Data mixing phase transitions.}
\citet{kazdan2025collapse} map the conditions under which synthetic data triggers collapse versus improves performance, \citet{gu2024cmr} establish a scaling law for critical mixture ratios in continual pre-training, and \citet{gu2025datamixing} show that data mixing can induce phase transitions in knowledge acquisition---a direct theoretical analogue to our sharp detection onset, where a critical mixing ratio separates qualitatively different detection regimes.

\paragraph{Critical transitions and lead-lag methodology.}
\citet{scheffer2009early} established that systems approaching tipping points exhibit generic early-warning signals (rising autocorrelation and variance); \citet{dakos2012methods} developed a practical toolkit for estimating such signals, while \citet{boettiger2012limits} quantified detection limits in short time series---directly relevant to iterative training where $T{=}5$--$20$.
The standard framework for testing temporal precedence is Granger causality~\cite{granger1969investigating}, with neural extensions for nonlinear settings~\citep{marcinkevics2022neural}; however, non-stationary series inflate rejection rates~\cite{granger1974spurious}, and the Toda--Yamamoto procedure~\cite{toda1995statistical} addresses this at reduced degrees of freedom severe in short series.
These methods are largely absent from the model collapse literature.

\paragraph{Analytical robustness.}
\citet{bell2022modeling} introduced multiverse analysis to ML, \citet{coretta2023multidimensional} demonstrated that 46 teams reached different conclusions on identical data, and \citet{kapoor2024reforms} synthesized these concerns into a consensus checklist.
Our work applies this perspective to model collapse: five methods across multiple contamination regimes produce conclusions ranging from universal significance to complete null.

\section*{Ethics Statement and AI Writing Disclosure}

\paragraph{AI writing assistance.}
This paper was drafted with assistance from Claude (Anthropic), used for LaTeX formatting, literature search, and iterative revision.
All scientific claims, experimental design decisions, data analysis, and interpretations are the sole responsibility of the authors.
The AI assistant did not design experiments, run code, or make editorial judgments about which results to include or exclude.

\paragraph{Responsible NLP considerations.}
This work studies degradation in iterative language model training.
We do not release models trained on synthetic data, as they exhibit reduced diversity and calibration.
Our findings highlight a failure mode relevant to the responsible deployment of language models: unmonitored iterative training on web-crawled data (which increasingly contains LLM outputs) may degrade model quality in ways detectable only through the multi-method framework we propose.
We encourage practitioners to implement canary monitoring in production training pipelines as a safeguard against silent quality degradation.

